  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nFriday and Saturday...\n\nBy the end of the week, baggy mid- and upper-level troughing will be \nin place over much of the Midwest, with ridging placed on either end \nof the trough across the intermountain West and the western North \nAtlantic. This will allow a sfc cold front to sink toward the region \nby Friday; however, the lack of amplification of the upper trough \nwill likely keep the front from making its way through the Ohio \nValley. Nevertheless, ahead of this boundary, ensemble mean PWATs \naround 1.8\" (~75th percentile of climatology) should keep a moist \nand unstable environment in place across the region. A more focused \nchance for showers and storms is favored to arise as one or two mid-\nlevel shortwaves/vort lobes rounds the base of the trough Friday \ninto Saturday. Timing will be important to the better chances for \nrain, as passage during peak heating would maximize shower/storm \nchances either day. Given somewhat better forcing, a strong storm or \ntwo can't be completely ruled out during this period, and there is a \nmodest signal in NCAR AI Severe Convection Guidance Friday into \nSaturday. Temperatures should trend fairly steady over this period, \nwith highs in the upper 80s to around 90 and lows in the upper 60s \nand low 70s.\n\nSunday through Early Next Week...\n\nA brief decrease in precipitation chances is expected for the second \nhalf of the weekend as the upper ridge over the western CONUS \nbroadens out. Height rises and slightly lower PWATs on Sunday should \nsuppress convective coverage, with temperatures remaining seasonably \nhot in the upper 80s and low 90s.\n\nAs we head into early-to-mid next week, a gradual retreat in the \npositive height anomalies is expected as persistent troughing over \nHudson Bay digs back to the southwest. How quickly this occurs is \nstill uncertain, but it will bring better shower/storm chances and a \nslight fall in temperatures to the area when it does happen. At this \ntime, the upper ridge is likely to continue to dominate on Monday, \nwith it potentially being the warmest day of the upcoming period \nwith widespread low 90s for highs. As we head into Tuesday and the \nmiddle of next week, the pattern should become more unsettled, with \nincreasing chances for (at least) afternoon and evening showers and \nstorms.",
